---
title: Introduction
description: What GateKeeper is, and the handful of concepts you need before configuring anything.
section: Getting started
---

GateKeeper is a **multi-tenant OAuth 2.1 / OpenID Connect identity provider**. You run it, your
applications delegate sign-in to it, and this dashboard is where you configure it.

These docs are the administrator's guide: how to create a tenant, register an application, model
who-can-do-what, send email, tune the risk engine, and answer a GDPR request — all from the
dashboard. They assume no prior knowledge of the codebase.

## The shape of the system

Almost every mistake people make with GateKeeper comes from misreading this hierarchy, so it is
worth thirty seconds:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Org["Organization<br/>owns admins and tenants"]
    T1["Tenant<br/>an OIDC issuer"]
    T2["Tenant<br/>another issuer"]
    U["Users"]
    G["Groups"]
    R["Roles"]
    C["OAuth Clients<br/>your apps"]
    E["Email, Legal, Risk policy"]

    Org --> T1
    Org --> T2
    T1 --> U
    T1 --> G
    T1 --> R
    T1 --> C
    T1 --> E
```

The load-bearing idea is the **tenant**.

A tenant is an *isolation boundary*, and specifically an **OIDC issuer**: it has its own ES256
signing keypair, its own users, and its own applications. A token minted for one tenant is not
valid in another — not "shouldn't be", *is not*, because the signature won't verify. Use separate
tenants for genuinely separate populations of users (staging vs production, or two unrelated
products). Do **not** use tenants as a way to group users inside one product — that is what groups
and roles are for.

## Two kinds of identity

This trips people up, so it is called out early. There are two completely separate populations,
and they do not mix:

| | Platform admins | End users |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Who | You and your colleagues | The people who sign into *your* apps |
| Sign in at | `/login` on the dashboard origin | The hosted login pages, per app |
| Scoped to | An organization | A single tenant |
| Token | HS256, admin-only | ES256, signed by the tenant's key |

An admin is not a user, and a user is not an admin. Creating a user in a tenant gives them no
access to this dashboard whatsoever.

> [!NOTE]
> There is no self-service admin signup. The first administrator is created from the command line
> — see [Before you begin](/docs/before-you-begin).

## What you can configure

Each of these has a chapter:

- **[OAuth clients](/docs/oauth-clients)** — your applications, their redirect URIs, scopes, and
  per-app MFA policy.
- **[Social login](/docs/social-login)** and **[branding](/docs/branding)** — sign in with Google
  or GitHub, and how the hosted login page looks.
- **[Users, groups & roles](/docs/users-groups-roles)** — who exists, and what lands in their token.
- **[Email](/docs/email)** — the SMTP server that sends verification and password-reset mail, and
  the templates for it.
- **[Security](/docs/risk-engine)** — adaptive risk scoring that can demand MFA or block a login
  outright.
- **[Compliance](/docs/audit-log)** — the audit trail, data-subject exports and erasure, and your
  terms/privacy pages.

## Where to start

If you have never used GateKeeper, read the next four pages in order. They take you from an empty
install to a real application performing a real login. Everything after that is reference you can
dip into.
